1
00:00:03,283 --> 00:00:05,183
Every day in Hong Kong,

2
00:00:05,183 --> 00:00:08,350
about four people die of liver cancer.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,483
That’s 1,412 people in 2022 alone.

4
00:00:13,717 --> 00:00:15,633
Here, it’s one of the three deadliest

5
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:19,483
cancers, alongside lung and colorectal.

6
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:21,117
And it’s not just us.

7
00:00:21,167 --> 00:00:23,817
Worldwide, it’s the sixth most common

8
00:00:23,817 --> 00:00:25,967
cancer, and the third deadliest.

9
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:27,633
Here is the thing.

10
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:29,400
We already have medicines.

11
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:30,933
Just not good enough.

12
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:32,967
The standard first treatment

13
00:00:32,967 --> 00:00:35,400
for advanced liver cancer has already

14
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:38,467
changed once, from Nexavar to a combo,

15
00:00:38,467 --> 00:00:41,683
atezolizumab plus bevacizumab.

16
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:42,933
But here,

17
00:00:42,933 --> 00:00:45,867
only about three in ten patients are alive

18
00:00:45,867 --> 00:00:47,133
five years on.

19
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:49,933
The study we follow calls it a huge

20
00:00:49,933 --> 00:00:51,133
unmet need.

21
00:00:51,617 --> 00:00:54,783
So… why not just make a better one?

22
00:00:55,233 --> 00:00:57,533
Because that’s way harder than it sounds.

23
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:00,733
Like, ten-to-seventeen-years hard.

24
00:01:01,167 --> 00:01:03,867
Start the search the year we were born,

25
00:01:03,867 --> 00:01:06,350
and it might only be ready now.

26
00:01:06,517 --> 00:01:10,500
It can cost almost 2.8 billion US dollars.

27
00:01:10,633 --> 00:01:13,117
And of drugs tested in people,

28
00:01:13,117 --> 00:01:16,567
only about one in ten reaches the market.

29
00:01:16,667 --> 00:01:20,067
To see why, let’s follow one drug’s journey.

30
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:23,300
Every medicine walks the same road.

31
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:24,750
Find a target.

32
00:01:24,767 --> 00:01:25,967
Find a hit,

33
00:01:25,967 --> 00:01:28,950
a first molecule that works on it.

34
00:01:29,067 --> 00:01:30,250
Improve it.

35
00:01:30,267 --> 00:01:33,350
Test it in cells, then animals.

36
00:01:33,617 --> 00:01:35,533
Then human trials:

37
00:01:35,533 --> 00:01:38,100
phase one, two, three.

38
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:39,300
Then approval.

39
00:01:39,317 --> 00:01:40,833
Any step can fail.

40
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:43,733
Now here’s where it gets interesting.

41
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:45,667
In 2024,

42
00:01:45,667 --> 00:01:47,767
part of the Nobel Prize in Chemistry

43
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:50,783
went to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper,

44
00:01:50,783 --> 00:01:52,200
for AlphaFold,

45
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,833
an AI that predicts the shape of proteins.

46
00:01:54,933 --> 00:01:58,050
So can AI walk the first steps faster?

47
00:01:58,133 --> 00:01:59,667
Let’s follow one real case,

48
00:01:59,667 --> 00:02:02,200
where AI tools took on liver cancer.

49
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:04,983
And part of it starts right here,

50
00:02:04,983 --> 00:02:06,000
in Hong Kong.

51
00:02:22,933 --> 00:02:24,267
Hong Kong first.

52
00:02:24,267 --> 00:02:26,067
In 2011, a team

53
00:02:26,067 --> 00:02:28,450
at the Chinese University of Hong Kong

54
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:30,650
studied liver tumours from patients

55
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:33,017
at the Prince of Wales Hospital.

56
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:35,333
One protein kept showing up raised:

57
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:38,733
CCRK, also called CDK20.

58
00:02:38,817 --> 00:02:40,867
Fast forward to 2023:

59
00:02:40,867 --> 00:02:42,900
an AI drug-discovery study goes

60
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,483
after the same protein,

61
00:02:44,483 --> 00:02:45,467
with co-authors

62
00:02:45,467 --> 00:02:48,050
at Insilico Medicine’s Hong Kong office

63
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:49,417
in Science Park.

64
00:02:49,933 --> 00:02:52,200
So our research question is:

65
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:55,000
how can computer-aided methods help

66
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,583
scientists find

67
00:02:56,583 --> 00:03:00,133
and assess potential cancer drug candidates?

68
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:03,933
And we will answer it with one case study:

69
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:06,283
CDK20 in liver cancer.

70
00:03:06,767 --> 00:03:09,817
We set ourselves two objectives.

71
00:03:10,350 --> 00:03:13,567
One: to identify the major computer-aided

72
00:03:13,567 --> 00:03:16,967
methods used in cancer drug discovery.

73
00:03:17,017 --> 00:03:20,833
Two: to evaluate how these methods help

74
00:03:20,833 --> 00:03:24,167
scientists assess potential drug candidates.

75
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:27,483
So how did we check all this? We read.

76
00:03:27,483 --> 00:03:28,283
A lot.

77
00:03:28,467 --> 00:03:30,333
No lab work of our own:

78
00:03:30,333 --> 00:03:32,150
this is a literature review.

79
00:03:32,367 --> 00:03:33,533
At the centre,

80
00:03:33,533 --> 00:03:35,267
one original study by Ren

81
00:03:35,267 --> 00:03:37,050
and colleagues in Chemical Science,

82
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:41,017
2023, with its supplementary data.

83
00:03:41,233 --> 00:03:44,317
Around it, the earlier Hong Kong study,

84
00:03:44,317 --> 00:03:46,150
papers behind two AI tools,

85
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:50,267
a 2023 review, a textbook chapter,

86
00:03:50,267 --> 00:03:53,383
and a 2026 expert Perspective,

87
00:03:53,383 --> 00:03:56,317
which we read only as an abstract.

88
00:03:56,417 --> 00:03:59,117
For every source, we asked:

89
00:03:59,117 --> 00:04:02,150
what kind is it, how much did we read,

90
00:04:02,150 --> 00:04:04,317
and how strong is the evidence?

91
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:05,833
Who did the work?

92
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:08,483
Were there controls and repeats?

93
00:04:08,567 --> 00:04:10,317
One thing worth knowing:

94
00:04:10,317 --> 00:04:13,317
most authors of the main study work

95
00:04:13,317 --> 00:04:15,717
for Insilico Medicine,

96
00:04:15,717 --> 00:04:18,483
which built its AI tools.

97
00:04:18,667 --> 00:04:20,483
They say so openly.

98
00:04:20,483 --> 00:04:24,067
Useful evidence, not an independent check.

99
00:04:24,167 --> 00:04:26,167
So here is where we are going.

100
00:04:26,467 --> 00:04:28,133
First, the lock.

101
00:04:28,783 --> 00:04:31,733
Then how AI helped cut a key for it.

102
00:04:32,233 --> 00:04:34,183
Then what happened in the lab.

103
00:04:34,467 --> 00:04:37,050
And finally, the big one:

104
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:39,383
how much did the computers really help?

105
00:04:39,983 --> 00:04:40,783
Okay.

106
00:04:40,817 --> 00:04:45,717
To get this, picture a lock and a key.

107
00:04:45,883 --> 00:04:47,500
Inside your cells,

108
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:50,033
proteins do a lot of the work.

109
00:04:50,233 --> 00:04:52,900
Most drugs grab one protein tightly

110
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:55,150
and change what it does,

111
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:57,683
slotting into a pocket like a key

112
00:04:57,683 --> 00:04:58,717
into a lock.

113
00:04:58,817 --> 00:05:01,883
Scientists call the lock the target,

114
00:05:01,883 --> 00:05:04,150
and the key a candidate molecule.

115
00:05:04,167 --> 00:05:06,883
A good key fits its own lock tightly,

116
00:05:06,883 --> 00:05:09,000
and opens no one else’s.

117
00:05:09,233 --> 00:05:10,917
That’s called selectivity.

118
00:05:11,017 --> 00:05:13,167
Our lock is CDK20.

119
00:05:13,283 --> 00:05:14,783
It’s an enzyme,

120
00:05:14,783 --> 00:05:16,950
a protein with a chemical job.

121
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,933
Its keyhole is a pocket where it normally

122
00:05:19,933 --> 00:05:22,633
grabs a small molecule called ATP.

123
00:05:23,050 --> 00:05:25,883
Plug that pocket with the right key,

124
00:05:25,883 --> 00:05:28,083
and the enzyme can’t do its job.

125
00:05:28,183 --> 00:05:29,183
Why this lock?

126
00:05:29,183 --> 00:05:30,800
Remember the Hong Kong team.

127
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:33,533
In 36 of 52 liver tumours,

128
00:05:33,533 --> 00:05:36,267
CDK20 was higher than in nearby

129
00:05:36,267 --> 00:05:37,667
non-tumour tissue.

130
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:40,450
High levels went with later-stage tumours

131
00:05:40,450 --> 00:05:41,900
and shorter survival.

132
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:45,550
And turning CDK20 down in liver cancer cells

133
00:05:45,550 --> 00:05:47,033
slowed their growth.

134
00:05:47,550 --> 00:05:51,267
So: it is a really strong suspect.

135
00:05:52,250 --> 00:05:55,650
Not the only cause of liver cancer,

136
00:05:55,650 --> 00:05:58,267
but a lock worth trying.

137
00:05:58,717 --> 00:06:00,783
In 2023,

138
00:06:00,783 --> 00:06:03,433
an AI platform called PandaOmics backed

139
00:06:03,433 --> 00:06:04,483
that hunch.

140
00:06:04,717 --> 00:06:07,050
It combed ten liver cancer data sets,

141
00:06:07,050 --> 00:06:08,850
over a thousand tumour samples

142
00:06:08,850 --> 00:06:10,667
and hundreds of healthy ones,

143
00:06:10,667 --> 00:06:12,383
plus research papers.

144
00:06:12,467 --> 00:06:14,950
People checked its top 20 by hand,

145
00:06:14,950 --> 00:06:17,683
and CDK20 came out on top,

146
00:06:17,683 --> 00:06:20,083
with no approved drug against it yet.

147
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:23,950
And no one had measured CDK20’s shape.

148
00:06:24,133 --> 00:06:26,717
That takes a lot of hard lab work,

149
00:06:26,717 --> 00:06:29,117
and only a small fraction of proteins

150
00:06:29,117 --> 00:06:31,050
have ever been measured.

151
00:06:31,267 --> 00:06:32,800
Without a 3D shape,

152
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:36,250
even computer predictions of binding get

153
00:06:36,250 --> 00:06:39,217
expensive, slow and difficult.

154
00:06:39,733 --> 00:06:42,317
So you’ve got a lock nobody can see.

155
00:06:42,917 --> 00:06:44,850
How do you cut a key in the dark?

156
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:11,867
the team did something

157
00:07:11,867 --> 00:07:14,850
that once sounded like science fiction.

158
00:07:15,067 --> 00:07:17,967
They asked an AI to guess the shape.

159
00:07:18,050 --> 00:07:21,317
Imagine a protein as a string of beads

160
00:07:21,317 --> 00:07:23,533
that folds into a 3D shape.

161
00:07:23,617 --> 00:07:26,183
The order of the beads is the sequence.

162
00:07:26,267 --> 00:07:29,033
AlphaFold learned from known structures

163
00:07:29,033 --> 00:07:30,483
how sequences fold.

164
00:07:30,533 --> 00:07:33,267
Give it one, and it predicts the shape,

165
00:07:33,267 --> 00:07:36,433
plus how sure it is about each part.

166
00:07:36,433 --> 00:07:39,317
Before, you’d need a lab-measured structure.

167
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:40,200
Now?

168
00:07:40,283 --> 00:07:42,967
The team just downloaded a prediction,

169
00:07:42,967 --> 00:07:45,983
for free, from a public database.

170
00:07:46,067 --> 00:07:47,367
According to the paper,

171
00:07:47,367 --> 00:07:49,800
this was the first time AlphaFold

172
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:53,083
had been used to find a hit like this.

173
00:07:53,183 --> 00:07:54,450
But look closer.

174
00:07:54,533 --> 00:07:56,383
One end of the protein,

175
00:07:56,383 --> 00:07:59,367
a floppy tail, had really low confidence.

176
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:02,350
And in the model, it lay across the keyhole,

177
00:08:02,350 --> 00:08:04,300
with one building block sitting

178
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:06,200
inside the ATP pocket.

179
00:08:06,417 --> 00:08:07,600
Trust it blindly,

180
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:09,917
and you’d cut keys for a blocked door.

181
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:12,083
So what did they do?

182
00:08:12,283 --> 00:08:13,617
They cut the tail off.

183
00:08:13,617 --> 00:08:15,033
The AI made the guess.

184
00:08:15,283 --> 00:08:17,067
A human caught the problem.

185
00:08:17,917 --> 00:08:20,900
And this next part honestly blew my mind.

186
00:08:21,117 --> 00:08:23,500
Think about how chemists have always found

187
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:24,333
a key.

188
00:08:24,417 --> 00:08:26,000
Imagine a molecule.

189
00:08:26,033 --> 00:08:26,883
Make it.

190
00:08:26,883 --> 00:08:27,883
Test it.

191
00:08:27,883 --> 00:08:28,950
It fails.

192
00:08:29,183 --> 00:08:29,983
Tweak it.

193
00:08:29,983 --> 00:08:31,150
Try again.

194
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:33,867
And every single round in a real lab costs

195
00:08:33,867 --> 00:08:35,250
time and money.

196
00:08:35,350 --> 00:08:37,483
Generative AI takes that loop

197
00:08:37,483 --> 00:08:39,617
and moves this into a computer.

198
00:08:39,867 --> 00:08:41,367
The platform here,

199
00:08:41,367 --> 00:08:44,817
Chemistry42, runs dozens of AI models

200
00:08:44,817 --> 00:08:48,017
side by side, built on known chemistry.

201
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:49,550
They invent new molecules

202
00:08:49,550 --> 00:08:51,483
for this exact keyhole,

203
00:08:51,483 --> 00:08:53,817
score them, learn from the scores,

204
00:08:53,817 --> 00:08:55,033
and go again.

205
00:08:55,333 --> 00:08:57,800
Its makers say it turns out new structures

206
00:08:57,800 --> 00:08:59,167
within hours.

207
00:08:59,683 --> 00:09:02,000
AI didn’t replace the scientists.

208
00:09:02,150 --> 00:09:04,667
It just ran their trial and error

209
00:09:04,667 --> 00:09:06,233
inside a computer,

210
00:09:06,233 --> 00:09:07,500
at light speed.

211
00:09:07,967 --> 00:09:10,517
The team gave it the pocket’s shape

212
00:09:10,517 --> 00:09:11,550
and one rule:

213
00:09:11,550 --> 00:09:14,567
every key must grip a spot called the hinge.

214
00:09:14,783 --> 00:09:17,967
Its filters are built to toss designs

215
00:09:17,967 --> 00:09:20,933
with reactive or possibly toxic groups,

216
00:09:20,933 --> 00:09:22,600
and to score how drug-like

217
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,767
and easy to make each one is.

218
00:09:39,867 --> 00:09:44,567
It came back with 8,918 brand-new designs.

219
00:09:44,683 --> 00:09:46,283
Way too many to make.

220
00:09:46,283 --> 00:09:49,217
So each key gets tried in a virtual lock.

221
00:09:49,433 --> 00:09:50,733
That’s docking:

222
00:09:50,733 --> 00:09:52,700
the computer predicts how a molecule

223
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:54,417
might sit in the pocket,

224
00:09:54,417 --> 00:09:55,850
and scores the fit.

225
00:09:55,933 --> 00:09:58,017
Similar designs get grouped,

226
00:09:58,017 --> 00:10:00,283
54 make the shortlist,

227
00:10:00,283 --> 00:10:02,650
and chemists pick just seven to make.

228
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:03,900
Wait.

229
00:10:04,500 --> 00:10:06,450
So the computer picked the winners?

230
00:10:06,450 --> 00:10:07,617
Not quite.

231
00:10:07,617 --> 00:10:10,383
A docking score is a clue,

232
00:10:10,383 --> 00:10:11,567
not proof.

233
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:13,233
Only the lab can tell.

234
00:10:13,417 --> 00:10:15,367
So, the moment of truth.

235
00:10:15,567 --> 00:10:17,417
Seven real molecules,

236
00:10:17,417 --> 00:10:19,083
made in a real lab.

237
00:10:19,333 --> 00:10:22,583
Do any actually grab CDK20?

238
00:10:22,867 --> 00:10:25,167
Grip is measured as Kd:

239
00:10:25,167 --> 00:10:29,033
roughly, how much molecule it takes to grab

240
00:10:29,033 --> 00:10:30,783
half the protein.

241
00:10:30,783 --> 00:10:32,300
Lower means tighter.

242
00:10:32,783 --> 00:10:34,817
Six options barely held up.

243
00:10:34,817 --> 00:10:36,567
But one option stood out.

244
00:10:36,567 --> 00:10:38,333
Its name is 001.

245
00:10:39,333 --> 00:10:43,317
001’s Kd: 9.2 micromolar.

246
00:10:43,317 --> 00:10:44,550
The other six?

247
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:46,283
All above 30.

248
00:10:46,283 --> 00:10:47,433
Much weaker.

249
00:10:47,683 --> 00:10:49,483
And here’s the wild part.

250
00:10:49,567 --> 00:10:52,033
The clock read just 30 days,

251
00:10:52,033 --> 00:10:54,817
from picking the target to this first

252
00:10:54,817 --> 00:10:55,833
confirmed hit.

253
00:10:55,933 --> 00:10:58,350
But then came a second test.

254
00:10:58,433 --> 00:11:01,817
Because grabbing a lock isn’t the same

255
00:11:01,817 --> 00:11:03,217
as jamming it.

256
00:11:03,567 --> 00:11:07,967
Does 001 actually stop CDK20 from working?

257
00:11:08,333 --> 00:11:11,583
That’s measured as IC50:

258
00:11:11,583 --> 00:11:14,333
how much you need to cut the enzyme’s

259
00:11:14,333 --> 00:11:15,800
activity in half.

260
00:11:15,883 --> 00:11:17,317
For 001?

261
00:11:17,317 --> 00:11:20,367
Above 6,000 nanomolar.

262
00:11:21,483 --> 00:11:23,550
The key went into the lock.

263
00:11:23,550 --> 00:11:25,467
But it barely turned.

264
00:11:25,467 --> 00:11:27,817
So that’s it?

265
00:11:27,817 --> 00:11:29,183
Back to square one?

266
00:11:29,633 --> 00:11:30,683
Not at all.

267
00:11:30,767 --> 00:11:34,267
This is where the loop kicks in again,

268
00:11:34,267 --> 00:11:37,083
now with real lab data inside.

269
00:11:37,083 --> 00:11:38,867
The results went back in,

270
00:11:38,867 --> 00:11:42,350
with a predicted pose of how 001 sat

271
00:11:42,350 --> 00:11:43,533
in the pocket.

272
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:45,133
Sixteen new designs.

273
00:11:45,133 --> 00:11:46,850
Six made and tested.

274
00:11:46,950 --> 00:11:50,000
All within 30 days of that first hit.

275
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:52,667
And one of them, 048,

276
00:11:52,667 --> 00:11:55,500
grabbed about fifteen times tighter.

277
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:58,133
Then the enzyme test.

278
00:11:58,283 --> 00:12:00,150
Remember, 001 needed

279
00:12:00,150 --> 00:12:03,717
more than 6,000 nanomolar.

280
00:12:03,867 --> 00:12:08,083
048 needed just 33.4.

281
00:12:08,567 --> 00:12:11,333
This time, the key turned.

282
00:12:19,650 --> 00:12:21,367
Then, the big step:

283
00:12:21,367 --> 00:12:22,500
living cells.

284
00:12:22,583 --> 00:12:25,883
After three days with 048,

285
00:12:25,883 --> 00:12:28,133
Huh7 liver cancer cells,

286
00:12:28,133 --> 00:12:30,350
packed with CDK20,

287
00:12:30,350 --> 00:12:33,517
lost half their living-cell signal

288
00:12:33,517 --> 00:12:37,033
at about 210 nanomolar.

289
00:12:37,217 --> 00:12:41,467
Comparison cells, HEK293,

290
00:12:41,467 --> 00:12:45,183
needed about 1,700.

291
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:46,933
Roughly eight times.

292
00:12:47,183 --> 00:12:48,933
So, the big question.

293
00:12:49,083 --> 00:12:52,067
How much did the computers actually help?

294
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:54,350
Let’s line up before and now.

295
00:12:54,450 --> 00:12:55,417
Seeing the lock.

296
00:12:55,417 --> 00:12:57,950
Before: hard lab experiments,

297
00:12:57,950 --> 00:12:59,600
and without a 3D shape,

298
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:01,083
design was expensive,

299
00:13:01,083 --> 00:13:02,600
slow and difficult.

300
00:13:02,617 --> 00:13:04,483
Now: a free prediction,

301
00:13:04,483 --> 00:13:06,933
downloaded from a public database.

302
00:13:07,033 --> 00:13:08,017
Finding a hit.

303
00:13:08,017 --> 00:13:09,683
Before: screening real compounds

304
00:13:09,683 --> 00:13:10,817
by the millions,

305
00:13:10,817 --> 00:13:14,017
an approach a 2023 review says has hit

306
00:13:14,017 --> 00:13:15,667
a costly bottleneck.

307
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:19,500
Now: 8,918 designs inside a computer,

308
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:21,850
and just 13 molecules actually made.

309
00:13:22,333 --> 00:13:24,850
Millions of compounds screened before.

310
00:13:25,250 --> 00:13:26,050
Here?

311
00:13:26,750 --> 00:13:27,933
Thirteen made.

312
00:13:27,933 --> 00:13:28,883
Thirteen.

313
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:30,950
That’s honestly wild.

314
00:13:31,417 --> 00:13:32,217
Time.

315
00:13:32,233 --> 00:13:35,250
Before: a ten-to-seventeen-year road,

316
00:13:35,250 --> 00:13:38,300
with finding a hit just the opening stretch.

317
00:13:38,300 --> 00:13:40,617
Now: 30 days to the first hit,

318
00:13:40,617 --> 00:13:42,883
and a better key within another 30.

319
00:13:42,983 --> 00:13:44,167
Improving the key.

320
00:13:44,167 --> 00:13:47,033
Before: every change meant another costly

321
00:13:47,033 --> 00:13:48,067
lab round.

322
00:13:48,067 --> 00:13:50,783
Now: a predicted picture of the first key

323
00:13:50,783 --> 00:13:52,950
guided sixteen designs,

324
00:13:52,950 --> 00:13:53,933
and the grip got

325
00:13:53,933 --> 00:13:55,983
about fifteen times tighter.

326
00:13:56,083 --> 00:13:57,033
To be fair,

327
00:13:57,033 --> 00:13:59,467
these pairs come from different studies:

328
00:13:59,467 --> 00:14:01,517
they compare scale and speed,

329
00:14:01,517 --> 00:14:03,917
not one race run side by side.

330
00:14:04,050 --> 00:14:07,117
But the paper itself calls the work cost-

331
00:14:07,117 --> 00:14:08,417
and time-efficient.

332
00:14:08,517 --> 00:14:10,067
Fewer failed molecules,

333
00:14:10,067 --> 00:14:12,733
lower costs, fewer animal tests,

334
00:14:12,733 --> 00:14:14,433
less chemical waste.

335
00:14:14,517 --> 00:14:16,767
Computing costs electricity too.

336
00:14:16,850 --> 00:14:18,217
And the real prize?

337
00:14:18,300 --> 00:14:19,583
New treatments for patients.

338
00:14:19,683 --> 00:14:22,883
So where’s 048 on the road?

339
00:14:22,900 --> 00:14:24,650
Target: found.

340
00:14:24,950 --> 00:14:26,467
Hit: found.

341
00:14:26,483 --> 00:14:28,700
Improving it: started.

342
00:14:29,117 --> 00:14:31,733
Cells: first tests, done.

343
00:14:31,750 --> 00:14:34,050
Animals and clinical trials:

344
00:14:34,050 --> 00:14:34,950
not yet.

345
00:14:34,967 --> 00:14:36,583
And here’s what the end of that road

346
00:14:36,583 --> 00:14:37,783
can look like.

347
00:14:37,800 --> 00:14:40,483
Some AI-assisted cancer drugs have walked

348
00:14:40,483 --> 00:14:41,533
much further.

349
00:14:41,550 --> 00:14:44,100
In September 2026,

350
00:14:44,100 --> 00:14:46,867
one of them, lirafugratinib,

351
00:14:46,867 --> 00:14:48,600
was approved in the US

352
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:50,450
for a bile duct cancer.

353
00:14:50,467 --> 00:14:52,617
Others failed along the way.

354
00:14:52,633 --> 00:14:53,950
And 048?

355
00:14:53,967 --> 00:14:57,183
Its makers chose not to take it to trials,

356
00:14:57,183 --> 00:14:58,017
and shared it

357
00:14:58,017 --> 00:14:59,850
with other researchers instead.

358
00:15:00,333 --> 00:15:02,950
048 is at the very start of the road.

359
00:15:03,100 --> 00:15:04,667
But for this case,

360
00:15:04,667 --> 00:15:05,867
that start took weeks,

361
00:15:05,867 --> 00:15:06,917
not years.

362
00:15:08,100 --> 00:15:10,083
So, what should happen next?

363
00:15:10,383 --> 00:15:12,550
We’d suggest three things.

364
00:15:13,117 --> 00:15:15,917
One: walk 048 further.

365
00:15:16,050 --> 00:15:18,850
It’s public now, so anyone could try.

366
00:15:19,033 --> 00:15:21,333
Test it in more liver cancer cells

367
00:15:21,333 --> 00:15:23,100
and normal liver cells,

368
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:24,367
then animals,

369
00:15:24,367 --> 00:15:26,417
and check how the body handles it

370
00:15:26,417 --> 00:15:27,983
and what else it hits.

371
00:15:28,183 --> 00:15:30,833
The authors had already listed these steps.

372
00:15:30,933 --> 00:15:33,233
Two: check the AlphaFold model

373
00:15:33,233 --> 00:15:35,633
against a lab-measured structure,

374
00:15:35,633 --> 00:15:37,067
once one exists.

375
00:15:37,233 --> 00:15:38,967
And feed every result,

376
00:15:38,967 --> 00:15:40,683
including the failures,

377
00:15:40,683 --> 00:15:42,350
back into the next round.

378
00:15:42,450 --> 00:15:44,283
Three: race them.

379
00:15:44,417 --> 00:15:47,433
An AI route and a classic route,

380
00:15:47,433 --> 00:15:49,617
same target, same budget,

381
00:15:49,617 --> 00:15:51,517
same definition of a hit.

382
00:15:51,800 --> 00:15:54,500
Then we’d know exactly how much faster AI

383
00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:55,317
really is.

384
00:15:55,817 --> 00:15:58,200
So, back to where we started.

385
00:15:58,650 --> 00:16:01,150
How can computer-aided methods help

386
00:16:01,150 --> 00:16:02,383
scientists find

387
00:16:02,383 --> 00:16:04,883
and assess cancer drug candidates?

388
00:16:04,967 --> 00:16:07,417
In this case, at four points.

389
00:16:07,450 --> 00:16:09,533
Picking a target from data.

390
00:16:09,683 --> 00:16:12,133
Predicting the lock’s shape.

391
00:16:12,350 --> 00:16:14,000
Designing new keys.

392
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,533
And ranking which ones to make.

393
00:16:16,533 --> 00:16:19,233
That’s our first objective,

394
00:16:19,233 --> 00:16:20,850
met for this case.

395
00:16:20,950 --> 00:16:22,333
And our second?

396
00:16:22,333 --> 00:16:25,133
These methods helped the team assess

397
00:16:25,133 --> 00:16:26,750
candidates fast.

398
00:16:26,833 --> 00:16:29,800
Screening by the millions became

399
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:31,617
thirteen molecules made.

400
00:16:31,700 --> 00:16:34,417
And on a road measured in years,

401
00:16:34,417 --> 00:16:36,683
the first hit came in 30 days,

402
00:16:36,683 --> 00:16:38,683
and round two made the key turn.

403
00:16:39,167 --> 00:16:40,833
This works in cells,

404
00:16:40,833 --> 00:16:42,817
not yet in people.

405
00:16:43,250 --> 00:16:45,583
The computer narrows the search.

406
00:16:46,433 --> 00:16:48,450
Experiments still decide.

407
00:16:48,567 --> 00:16:50,533
So next time you see a headline

408
00:16:50,533 --> 00:16:53,250
like ‘AI discovers a cancer drug’…

409
00:16:53,367 --> 00:16:56,900
…ask where it really is on the road:

410
00:16:56,900 --> 00:16:59,650
computer, protein,

411
00:16:59,650 --> 00:17:03,633
cells, animals, or patients?

412
00:17:03,650 --> 00:17:05,900
AI didn’t replace the scientists.

413
00:17:06,100 --> 00:17:09,167
It gave them trial and error at light speed.

414
00:17:09,583 --> 00:17:10,950
Thanks for watching.

